      title: "The Jōkyū War and Warrior Law",
      body: `## The court strikes back

The retired emperor **Go-Toba** (1180–1239), who had been placed on the throne as a child during the Genpei War, was a man of great energy and many talents: a poet who sponsored the eighth imperial anthology, the *Shin kokinshū*, an accomplished swordsmith, horseman, and archer. He was determined to restore the authority of the court over the warriors. After the assassination of the shōgun Sanetomo in 1219, he saw his opportunity. In 1221, he declared the Hōjō regent **Hōjō Yoshitoki** a rebel and called on the warriors of the country to destroy him.

The result was the **Jōkyū War**, named for the era in which it took place. It lasted about a month. The eastern warriors, rallied by Masako, marched on Kyoto in overwhelming numbers, and the court's forces collapsed. Go-Toba was exiled to the remote Oki Islands in the Sea of Japan, where he spent the last eighteen years of his life writing poetry; two other retired emperors were also exiled, and the reigning emperor was deposed. The bakufu confiscated some three thousand estates belonging to the court's supporters and distributed their stewardships to its own followers, extending its presence into western Japan, where it had been weak. It established a headquarters in Kyoto, at **Rokuhara**, headed by members of the Hōjō family, to watch the court and administer the west.

The Jōkyū War marked a decisive shift in the balance between court and warriors. The court would never again be able to challenge the shogunate by force until the fourteenth century. Henceforth, the bakufu would supervise the imperial succession and approve the selection of emperors.

## The Goseibai Shikimoku

With the warriors now spread across the country, disputes over land multiplied: between stewards and estate owners, between warriors and their kinsmen, between neighbors. In 1232, the regent **Hōjō Yasutoki** issued a code of law for the warriors: the **Goseibai Shikimoku**, "Formulary of Adjudications," a short code of fifty-one articles. Unlike the ritsuryō codes, which were modeled on Chinese law, the Shikimoku was based on the customs of the warriors and the precedents of Kamakura's own judgments since the time of Yoritomo.

Yasutoki explained its purpose in a letter to his brother at Rokuhara. The laws of the court, he wrote, were written in Chinese characters that warriors could not read, and they were applied inconsistently; the new code was written so that even those who knew only kana could understand it, so that judgments would be impartial and warriors would know in advance what the law required. It did not replace the law of the court, which continued to apply to the court and its estates, but it governed the warriors and their disputes.

The Shikimoku dealt with the duties of military governors and stewards, the inheritance and transfer of land, the rights of wives and daughters, the treatment of runaways, and the procedures of lawsuits. It upheld the principle that land held peaceably for twenty years could not be challenged. It allowed women to inherit land and hold the office of steward, and permitted a woman divorced by her husband without cause to keep land he had given her. It became the foundation of warrior law for centuries, and it was used as a primer for teaching children to read until the nineteenth century.

## Justice at Kamakura

The bakufu became famous for the fairness of its courts. Litigants submitted written claims and counter-claims, supported by documents—deeds, wills, letters of appointment—and the Board of Inquiry examined them and handed down written judgments. Thousands of these documents survive, and they form the richest source for the social history of the period. They show warriors suing their brothers over inheritances, stewards accused of seizing estate income, and estate owners petitioning against warriors who refused to pay dues. They also show women litigating vigorously for their rights.

The documents reveal a society in which warrior families divided their lands among all their children, sons and daughters alike, under the authority of the family head. This practice of **divided inheritance** would cause serious problems over time. As lands were split among heirs generation after generation, holdings shrank, and many warrior families fell into poverty. By the late thirteenth century, warrior families increasingly passed their lands to a single heir, and daughters' rights to inherit were reduced, often to a life interest that returned to the main line on their death. The rights of women, which had been considerable in the Kamakura period, declined in the centuries that followed.

## The way of the bow and horse

What did it mean to be a warrior in Kamakura Japan? The word **bushidō**, "the way of the warrior," which later became famous, was not used in this period; it was popularized only in the seventeenth century, and above all in the twentieth. Kamakura warriors spoke instead of "the way of the bow and horse" (*kyūba no michi*), and their ideals were practical: skill in mounted archery, courage, loyalty to one's lord—so long as he rewarded his followers—and concern for the honor and prosperity of one's house. Warriors could and did change sides; loyalty was a relationship of mutual obligation, not an absolute. The romantic ideal of the samurai who would die for his lord without question was largely a creation of later centuries, though the war tales of the Kamakura period supplied it with heroes.

Kamakura warriors were also eager to acquire the culture of the court. The third shōgun, Sanetomo, was a fine poet; Hōjō regents collected books, sponsored temples, and invited Chinese Zen monks to Kamakura. Warrior culture was not opposed to court culture; it gradually absorbed it.

> **Pause and consider.** Hōjō Yasutoki wrote his code in a form warriors could read, so that judgments would be impartial and predictable. Why might written, public law have mattered so much to a warrior society?`,
