      title: "The Fall of Kamakura",
      body: `## Strains on the bakufu

By the early fourteenth century, the Kamakura bakufu was in serious trouble. The cost of the Mongol invasions and the decades of coastal defense that followed had impoverished many warriors, who received few rewards. Divided inheritance had shrunk their holdings. The growth of a money economy, using Chinese copper coins imported in great quantities, drew warriors into debt to moneylenders, often associated with temples or with the new merchant class. In 1297, the bakufu issued a **decree of virtuous government** (*tokusei*), canceling the debts of gokenin and ordering the return of lands they had sold or pawned; but the measure made lenders reluctant to lend to warriors at all, and it was partly withdrawn.

At the same time, the Hōjō had concentrated power in the hands of the main line of the family, the **tokusō**, and its private retainers, excluding other gokenin families from influence. Many warriors who had once seen the bakufu as their protector now saw it as the private government of the Hōjō. In the provinces, bands of lawless men called **akutō**, "evil bands"—often local warriors, estate managers, and others who resisted the authority of estate owners and of Kamakura—seized harvests and attacked estates. The bakufu struggled to suppress them, and its failure to keep order in the provinces undermined the claim on which its authority rested: that it alone could protect the lands and rights of those who served it.

## A divided imperial house

The bakufu also became entangled in a dispute within the imperial family. In the second half of the thirteenth century, the imperial line split into two branches, the **Jimyōin** and the **Daikakuji**, each descended from a son of an emperor of the 1240s, and each claiming the throne. Kamakura, as the arbiter of the imperial succession, decided in 1317 that the two lines should alternate on the throne, each emperor reigning for about ten years. The arrangement satisfied no one, and each branch lobbied Kamakura constantly to shorten the other's reigns.

## Go-Daigo

In 1318, a member of the Daikakuji line, **Go-Daigo** (1288–1339), came to the throne. He was a man of strong will and wide learning, who had studied the Neo-Confucian ideas newly arrived from Song China, with their emphasis on the loyalty owed by ministers to their sovereign. He was determined not only to keep the throne for his own line but to restore the direct rule of the emperor, as in the age before the regents and shōguns. He took as his model the reign of Emperor **Daigo** in the early tenth century, idealized as a golden age of imperial rule, and chose his own posthumous name, Go-Daigo, "the later Daigo," in tribute.

Go-Daigo plotted against the bakufu twice, enlisting monks, courtiers, and warriors from outside the gokenin network. The first plot, in 1324, was discovered, and his associates were punished. The second, in 1331, led to open revolt; Go-Daigo fled the capital with the regalia, was captured, and was exiled in 1332 to the Oki Islands, where Go-Toba had been exiled a century before. But his son, Prince **Moriyoshi**, and a warrior from the region south of Nara, **Kusunoki Masashige**, kept up a guerrilla war against the bakufu from mountain fortresses, and warriors discontented with the Hōjō began to rally to the imperial cause. Kusunoki, who held his fortress of Chihaya against a vast besieging army with rocks, logs, and boiling water, would become in later centuries the model of the loyal subject who serves his emperor unto death.

## 1333

In 1333, Go-Daigo escaped from Oki. The bakufu sent its leading general, **Ashikaga Takauji** (1305–1358), head of a powerful gokenin family descended from the Minamoto, to crush him. Instead, Takauji changed sides and attacked the bakufu's headquarters at Rokuhara in Kyoto, destroying it. At almost the same moment, in the east, another Minamoto descendant, **Nitta Yoshisada**, marched on Kamakura itself. According to legend, he threw his sword into the sea at the cape of Inamuragasaki, praying to the sea god; the tide withdrew, and his army marched along the exposed beach into the city. After days of fighting in the narrow valleys that had made Kamakura easy to defend, the city fell. The last Hōjō regent and several hundred members of his family and retainers took their own lives at the family temple of Tōshōji. The Kamakura shogunate, which had ruled Japan for a century and a half, was destroyed in a few weeks.

## Assessing Kamakura

The Kamakura period established the pattern of Japanese government for the next five centuries: a warrior government, ruling in the name of an emperor who reigned without ruling. It created a body of warrior law, the Goseibai Shikimoku, that endured into the Edo period. It saw the defeat of the Mongol invasions, which shaped Japanese ideas of divine protection for seven hundred years. It produced the *Tale of the Heike* and the *Hōjōki*, the founders of the new Buddhist schools, and the beginnings of Zen culture. It saw the spread of a money economy and of new markets, and the first signs of the village communities that would organize themselves in later centuries. And it saw the warrior class grow from provincial retainers of the court aristocracy to the rulers of the country, though they had not yet entirely displaced the court and its estates.

Go-Daigo now set out to rule Japan himself. His attempt, the **Kenmu Restoration**, would last less than three years, and its failure would open an age of civil war that continued, in one form or another, for most of the next two and a half centuries. That age is the subject of the next lesson.

> **Pause and consider.** The bakufu was destroyed not by the court but by its own leading general, who changed sides. What does the fall of Kamakura reveal about the nature of loyalty among the warriors?`,
